strings)\n2. Extract calls = case[\"payload\"][\"calls\"] (list of dicts)\n3. Validate: each call must have call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. Assume valid.\n4. Sort calls by (-score, start, call_id) (using tuple)\n5. Greedy selection:\n   - selected = []\n   - for call in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = any(not (call['end'] <= sel['start'] or call['start'] >= sel['end']) for sel in selected)\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n6. Sort selected by start ascending for reporting: selected_sorted_by_start = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'])\n   Then selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected_sorted_by_start]\n7. Apply replacements right to left:\n   - result_tokens = list(tokens) (copy)\n   - Sort selected by start descending (or end descending) to apply from rightmost to leftmost.\n   - For call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True):\n        start = call['start']\n        end = call['end']\n        replacement = call['replacement_tokens']\n        # replace slice [start:end] with replacement list\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:start] + replacement + result_tokens[end:]\n   - Note: This works because we go from rightmost to leftmost; the indices of earlier spans remain untouched.\n8. Return {\"selected_ids\": selected_ids, \"interleaved_tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nEdge cases: No calls, overlapping selection, replacement_tokens may be empty? Possibly.\n\nMake sure to handle that start and end are integers within bounds of tokens? Assume valid.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is a JSON string containing the Python source. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's produce the Python code as a single string. We'll write a function solve(case) that returns the dict. Use proper indentation.\n\nWe'll ensure the function uses only standard library.\n\nNow write the JSON string."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tok